WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:04.540
SUNIL RAYAN: My
name is Sunil Rayan.

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:08.290
I lead Google Cloud for
Games team here at Google.

00:00:08.290 --> 00:00:11.200
And it's an honor for
me to be here today.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.790
As you can imagine
and see, Google

00:00:13.790 --> 00:00:16.329
has announced some really
big things this week.

00:00:16.329 --> 00:00:21.342
So I'm sure many of you attended
Mobile Developer Day on Monday.

00:00:21.342 --> 00:00:23.050
And if you haven't,
you should definitely

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:24.830
check out the Tuesday keynote.

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:27.580
We announced a big
platform called Stadia.

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:30.850
And today, for the
first time, we actually

00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:34.270
have an entire day dedicated
to Google Cloud developers.

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:38.030
And we've got a fantastic
lineup of speakers,

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:40.090
both subject matter
experts from Google

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:42.910
but more importantly,
customers and partners

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:46.820
that are going to talk about how
Google Cloud is helping power

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:48.070
their games.

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:50.620
But before we get
into this, I want

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:54.400
to take a step back and talk
a little bit about the trends

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:57.460
that we're seeing and how it
affects our approach in Google

00:00:57.460 --> 00:00:58.990
Cloud.

00:00:58.990 --> 00:01:03.640
You all know there has been an
amazing explosion of players

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:05.410
globally-- about
2 billion, which

00:01:05.410 --> 00:01:07.540
means there's huge opportunity.

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:09.910
And the industry is growing
at a tremendous rate.

00:01:09.910 --> 00:01:12.100
And this is all thanks
to people like you

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:15.507
who, with your creativity,
are building fantastic games.

00:01:15.507 --> 00:01:16.090
So that's one.

00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:18.160
So we're dealing
with massive scale.

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:21.430
Second, it's just not
about players, right?

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:26.980
The business of games took about
35 years to get to $35 billion

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:28.360
in revenue.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:31.890
But then in the
subsequent 11 years,

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:34.160
we've added another
$100 billion annually.

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:36.610
So it's not just there
are lots of players.

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:38.630
There's a lot of
money in this market.

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:41.350
And it's kind of becoming
the biggest entertainment

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:42.920
industry out there.

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:43.930
So that's number two.

00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:46.870
Because we are seeing ways
for people to monetize.

00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:48.970
Number three, what
we are also seeing

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:52.000
is with the
democratization of devices,

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:54.610
you have to reach
a player base that

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:57.730
is of different sizes and shapes
and different screen sizes.

00:01:57.730 --> 00:02:00.010
And what people
are starting to see

00:02:00.010 --> 00:02:05.730
is with 5G coming and low
latency in different areas,

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:08.440
AAA games or mobile games
are thinking that people are

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:10.490
going to be platform agnostic.

00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:13.200
So you need to start building
for different sizes and shapes

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.610
and some of these things
that we talked about even

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:17.560
in yesterday's keynote
on Stadia, that you can

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:20.280
play across different devices.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.770
Lastly, as a trend
that we're also seeing

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:26.760
is a game is just
not a game anymore.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:28.380
It's becoming an ecosystem.

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:32.640
You have successful games that
have more developers creating

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:33.570
content.

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:35.760
We have social
connections, and things

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:37.840
can explode from day one.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:40.380
So it's really hard to
forecast what's going

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:43.210
to happen and prepare for it.

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:46.770
And the second thing is, as
a result, games' lifetime

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:48.780
is becoming longer
and longer because you

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:50.250
have ways to update content.

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:52.320
So the new normal,
pretty much, right

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:57.210
now is to you need to
constantly develop and iterate.

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:59.410
And that's the new normal
for building games.

00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:02.110
So these are the trends that
we took into account and said,

00:03:02.110 --> 00:03:07.250
OK, how does that affect
what the Google Cloud game

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:10.170
strategy should be and how
we can help developers?

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:13.280
And after talking
to a lot of you,

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:15.500
we also heard there
are four things

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:17.835
that we need, taking
the trends into account

00:03:17.835 --> 00:03:18.960
and what we heard from you.

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:22.590
One, you want scale from day one
if you want to be successful.

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:25.170
You don't want any obstacles
when you launch a game to say,

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:27.750
oops, I couldn't scale, so I'm
not going to be successful.

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:28.670
So that's one.

00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:32.695
Number two, with all
the different devices

00:03:32.695 --> 00:03:34.070
and with the
different platforms,

00:03:34.070 --> 00:03:36.580
you want flexibility so you
can build once and deploy it

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:38.610
in as many places as you want.

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:43.760
Number three, you also said, I
want less operational overhead,

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:46.130
and I just want to focus
on building good games.

00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:49.130
And lastly, given
you never know what

00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:50.960
you can expect in
terms of players,

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:54.470
you want better ability
to predict outcomes.

00:03:54.470 --> 00:03:56.525
You want better ability
to forecast where players

00:03:56.525 --> 00:03:57.650
are coming from, et cetera.

00:03:57.650 --> 00:04:00.380
So predictability is
becoming a core need

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:02.570
for a lot of game
developers, as well.

00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:04.460
So we took this into
account and said,

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:07.800
what does Google Cloud for
Games actually stand for?

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:11.720
So one-- you will hear a lot
about this from us throughout

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:12.770
the rest of day--

00:04:12.770 --> 00:04:15.500
one, we want to enable
cross-platform services

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:16.940
and solutions.

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:19.709
Number two, we are
committed to open source.

00:04:19.709 --> 00:04:22.490
You will see in
subsequent sessions

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:23.990
that we'll talk
a little bit more

00:04:23.990 --> 00:04:26.150
about how we are partnering
with the industry

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:28.070
to push the thought
leadership forward.

00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:31.010
And we want to give
developers choice.

00:04:31.010 --> 00:04:36.530
Number three, Google has been
used to having lots of data

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:40.890
and building analytics,
predictive algorithms.

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:42.370
Machine learning
is becoming core.

00:04:42.370 --> 00:04:45.890
So you will see a lot of machine
learning services from Google.

00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:48.290
And lastly, if you haven't
seen the commitment,

00:04:48.290 --> 00:04:50.900
I would say for the
first time we have--

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:53.720
the number of Googlers
at GDC is exploding.

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:56.120
And that's because we have
a commitment for games.

00:04:56.120 --> 00:04:58.880
And what that
results in is we want

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:02.660
to bring the best of Google to
you across different product

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:04.430
areas and different solutions.

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:08.050
And you will hear some of
that later today, as well.

00:05:08.050 --> 00:05:11.260
Let me dive a little
bit into it to say

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:12.520
this is not just in theory.

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:16.480
We actually have top AAA
games and mobile games

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:17.710
running on Google Cloud.

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:21.787
This is just a sample
list of game titles,

00:05:21.787 --> 00:05:23.620
as well as partners
that we're working with,

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:26.440
like Multiplay and
Unity and Improbable,

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:32.840
that's helping advance what
Cloud can do for your games.

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:35.200
So diving a little bit
deeper into our strategy,

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:38.140
one of the things that
we have committed to

00:05:38.140 --> 00:05:40.080
is giving developers choice.

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:42.250
In a lot of things
in Google Cloud,

00:05:42.250 --> 00:05:44.300
we have this platform
called Kubernetes,

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:45.880
which is based on containers.

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:49.330
And we feel like that's
something that gives choice

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:53.110
because you can run Kubernetes
on Google Cloud, on-premise,

00:05:53.110 --> 00:05:55.702
on your bare metal, as
well as on other clouds.

00:05:55.702 --> 00:05:57.910
And we're just not committed
to this in Google Cloud.

00:05:57.910 --> 00:06:00.130
We're committed to that
in gaming in Google Cloud,

00:06:00.130 --> 00:06:00.970
as well.

00:06:00.970 --> 00:06:03.310
And some of the examples
that we have partnered

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:07.120
with the industry to push
that thinking on is, one,

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:09.135
we've got a open-source
project called Agones

00:06:09.135 --> 00:06:10.510
that we launched
about a year ago

00:06:10.510 --> 00:06:13.930
that you'll hear more of which
automatically helps you scale

00:06:13.930 --> 00:06:15.640
your game server processes.

00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:17.140
So you don't have
to worry about it.

00:06:17.140 --> 00:06:18.790
And it also gives
you a good dashboard

00:06:18.790 --> 00:06:23.110
of metrics on how servers
are scaling up and down.

00:06:23.110 --> 00:06:26.410
We've also-- again, with an
industry partner, with Unity--

00:06:26.410 --> 00:06:29.860
launched Open Match, which
is a matchmaking service.

00:06:29.860 --> 00:06:30.700
It's open source.

00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:34.250
Anybody could take and use it
and put in your specific logic

00:06:34.250 --> 00:06:36.010
in to make matchmaking work.

00:06:36.010 --> 00:06:38.560
So we're just not
saying open source.

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:41.020
We are actually doing
specific projects

00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:44.150
that are related to games.

00:06:44.150 --> 00:06:48.230
It's also not just that we
are funding these projects.

00:06:48.230 --> 00:06:50.300
We are also making it
easy for you to access it.

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:54.640
And as of today, you can use
Agones with a simple click

00:06:54.640 --> 00:06:57.460
from the Cloud Marketplace
and embed it into your game.

00:06:57.460 --> 00:07:00.520
So this is something that
is very exciting for us

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:04.490
and helps advance Kubernetes and
game servers into the industry,

00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:04.990
as well.

00:07:07.970 --> 00:07:10.570
The next part in
our strategy is we

00:07:10.570 --> 00:07:14.350
want you to get results fast
and get more player insights.

00:07:14.350 --> 00:07:17.290
Because you want to improve
the player experience,

00:07:17.290 --> 00:07:20.240
as well as get more
insight from your players.

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:24.070
And we've partnered
with lots of-- sorry,

00:07:24.070 --> 00:07:25.495
I flipped the slide.

00:07:25.495 --> 00:07:26.980
Can you go back one?

00:07:26.980 --> 00:07:29.570
We've also partnered
with a lot of companies--

00:07:29.570 --> 00:07:32.150
big companies which have
humongous amounts of data,

00:07:32.150 --> 00:07:33.700
like 50 petabytes--

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:35.680
with people like
King and Square Enix,

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:38.320
to bring more insights
about your players so

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:40.390
you can improve the
design of your game

00:07:40.390 --> 00:07:43.270
or improve the
monetization of your game.

00:07:43.270 --> 00:07:47.828
On infrastructure, we also
partnered very closely with

00:07:47.828 --> 00:07:50.370
Massive Entertainment studios--
can you go to the next slide,

00:07:50.370 --> 00:07:51.450
please?--

00:07:51.450 --> 00:07:54.400
to help launch "Tom
Clancy Division 2."

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:57.460
And we have a model where
we our custom engineers

00:07:57.460 --> 00:08:00.740
were in the war room to
make sure this launch was

00:08:00.740 --> 00:08:01.240
successful.

00:08:01.240 --> 00:08:05.180
This actually launched
on Friday last week.

00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:08.340
So this is a little bit
of what we talked about.

00:08:08.340 --> 00:08:11.050
And lastly, I'll
finish on our strategy.

00:08:11.050 --> 00:08:13.090
We want to bring
the best of Google.

00:08:13.090 --> 00:08:16.520
We want to help the
game industry succeed.

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:18.090
We want to help
developers succeed.

00:08:18.090 --> 00:08:22.100
And we feel Google Cloud is
the glue to those efforts.

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:26.950
And with that, we want to work
with different Google teams

00:08:26.950 --> 00:08:28.330
to bring this to life.

00:08:28.330 --> 00:08:31.300
And on that note, I'm sure you
heard about Stadia yesterday.

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:34.980
And let me bring up
our next speakers, who

00:08:34.980 --> 00:08:38.230
are going to discuss how Stadia
and GCP, and Google Cloud

00:08:38.230 --> 00:08:40.468
Platform, are actually
better together.

00:08:40.468 --> 00:08:43.342
Micah?

00:08:43.342 --> 00:08:46.695
[APPLAUSE]

00:08:48.620 --> 00:08:50.448
MICAH BAKER: Thank you, Sunil.

00:08:50.448 --> 00:08:50.990
Good morning.

00:08:50.990 --> 00:08:51.657
I'm Micah Baker.

00:08:51.657 --> 00:08:55.070
I'm a product manager for
gaming on Google Cloud.

00:08:55.070 --> 00:08:57.980
And I'm joined by my
colleague, Pawel, who

00:08:57.980 --> 00:09:00.020
is a product manager on Stadia.

00:09:00.020 --> 00:09:02.570
And I'm sure you would love
to hear a little bit more

00:09:02.570 --> 00:09:04.580
about that exciting
announcement from yesterday.

00:09:04.580 --> 00:09:05.810
And so I'm going to
hand it over to Pawel

00:09:05.810 --> 00:09:07.250
to talk a little
bit about Stadia.

00:09:07.250 --> 00:09:10.340
And then, I'll talk about how
Google Cloud and Stadia work

00:09:10.340 --> 00:09:13.225
better together.

00:09:13.225 --> 00:09:16.040
PAWEL SIARKIEWICZ: Thank you.

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:18.700
So Stadia was
announced yesterday.

00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:21.930
Who's seen the keynote or has
heard a little bit about it?

00:09:21.930 --> 00:09:22.430
OK.

00:09:22.430 --> 00:09:23.180
Fair number.

00:09:23.180 --> 00:09:28.540
So as a quick summary, Stadia
is a new kind of platform.

00:09:28.540 --> 00:09:32.560
It's a cloud-based gaming
platform for AAA gaming

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:34.390
that is based on streaming.

00:09:34.390 --> 00:09:36.310
And it is a new
platform that lets

00:09:36.310 --> 00:09:38.230
you build new kinds of games.

00:09:38.230 --> 00:09:40.630
But we have built it to
be intuitive and work

00:09:40.630 --> 00:09:41.710
with all kinds of games.

00:09:41.710 --> 00:09:45.530
And so we're very excited
about what we can bring to it.

00:09:45.530 --> 00:09:47.030
And what makes it
unique, of course,

00:09:47.030 --> 00:09:50.950
is that because it is hosted
in Cloud and it streams,

00:09:50.950 --> 00:09:54.280
it can connect to different
devices and different endpoints

00:09:54.280 --> 00:09:56.470
without having to change
the core game experience

00:09:56.470 --> 00:09:58.270
or how you build it.

00:09:58.270 --> 00:10:00.770
We're launching in 2019.

00:10:00.770 --> 00:10:03.860
And the reason this is
relevant to this audience,

00:10:03.860 --> 00:10:06.440
specifically, for
the Google Cloud

00:10:06.440 --> 00:10:09.410
is that whenever your
game is running on Stadia,

00:10:09.410 --> 00:10:11.660
it's always running in a
virtualized environment

00:10:11.660 --> 00:10:15.310
on a specialized VM that
we call an instance.

00:10:15.310 --> 00:10:19.330
And these instances connect to
the endpoints through a stream,

00:10:19.330 --> 00:10:22.270
through the internet
and Wi-Fi and whatever

00:10:22.270 --> 00:10:24.600
the player is playing on.

00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:27.260
But the part that is
important to remember for here

00:10:27.260 --> 00:10:32.000
is that the Stadia
instances aren't generic GCP

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:32.790
virtual machines.

00:10:32.790 --> 00:10:33.770
They are specialized.

00:10:33.770 --> 00:10:37.860
And so you will interact with
them in the Stadia environment.

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:39.592
Now, when players
are playing, we

00:10:39.592 --> 00:10:40.800
take care of those instances.

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:43.190
So once you launch your game,
when players are playing,

00:10:43.190 --> 00:10:45.660
we manage all of that, so you
don't have to worry about it.

00:10:45.660 --> 00:10:48.077
But when you're in development
and you have your developer

00:10:48.077 --> 00:10:50.480
instances, then you will
be interacting with them

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:52.153
in our environment.

00:10:52.153 --> 00:10:54.070
And this is where some
of the power of the GCP

00:10:54.070 --> 00:10:57.760
can come in because all of
the Stadia infrastructure

00:10:57.760 --> 00:11:01.510
is running on the same
backbone that GCP is running.

00:11:01.510 --> 00:11:07.470
And so the connection to GCP
is very fast and efficient.

00:11:07.470 --> 00:11:09.820
And to talk a little
bit more about that,

00:11:09.820 --> 00:11:11.815
I'll pass it on to Micah.

00:11:11.815 --> 00:11:13.930
MICAH BAKER: Thank you.

00:11:13.930 --> 00:11:17.560
So now that you understand a
little bit of the logistics

00:11:17.560 --> 00:11:21.490
of Stadia, it's interesting
to compare how that fits in

00:11:21.490 --> 00:11:23.420
with the Google Cloud story.

00:11:23.420 --> 00:11:26.810
Google Cloud has a unique
global private network,

00:11:26.810 --> 00:11:29.650
which is really fundamental
in this better value

00:11:29.650 --> 00:11:31.377
that we're going
to talk about here.

00:11:31.377 --> 00:11:33.460
So you think about all
these different connections

00:11:33.460 --> 00:11:34.510
across the globe.

00:11:34.510 --> 00:11:36.760
And having a private
network really matters

00:11:36.760 --> 00:11:39.040
when you want to
control latency.

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:41.890
What does this do to
Stadia, for example?

00:11:48.830 --> 00:11:51.620
So when you work with
Google Cloud and Stadia,

00:11:51.620 --> 00:11:54.470
that private network
actually comes in very handy

00:11:54.470 --> 00:11:57.440
because we can have
all the traffic local.

00:11:57.440 --> 00:12:00.230
The traffic never has to
go through the internet,

00:12:00.230 --> 00:12:05.260
as you may experience
through other clouds.

00:12:05.260 --> 00:12:08.500
What happens if your network
traffic gets to stay local,

00:12:08.500 --> 00:12:09.340
though?

00:12:09.340 --> 00:12:13.180
What does that bring to your
game and to your experience?

00:12:13.180 --> 00:12:15.833
It really comes down to
these three main points.

00:12:15.833 --> 00:12:17.750
Your traffic is never
exposed to the internet,

00:12:17.750 --> 00:12:19.550
so no one can mess
with your traffic.

00:12:19.550 --> 00:12:21.750
The game data is
going to be secure.

00:12:21.750 --> 00:12:23.240
You can count on
the performance.

00:12:23.240 --> 00:12:26.180
It's predictably low
latency because it's always

00:12:26.180 --> 00:12:27.710
on that private
network where we can

00:12:27.710 --> 00:12:29.835
control the quality of service.

00:12:29.835 --> 00:12:31.460
It's also a great
value because there's

00:12:31.460 --> 00:12:35.830
no charge for egressing to
the internet from the cloud.

00:12:35.830 --> 00:12:37.780
So we're really excited
about the combination

00:12:37.780 --> 00:12:40.412
of Stadia and Google Cloud.

00:12:40.412 --> 00:12:42.370
And we look forward to
talking more about this.

00:12:42.370 --> 00:12:47.710
This was just a little sneak
peek into what we're thinking.

00:12:47.710 --> 00:12:50.470
And next, I'm going to
hand it over to Pawel

00:12:50.470 --> 00:12:52.150
to talk a little bit
about what you can

00:12:52.150 --> 00:12:53.912
do to learn more about Stadia.

00:12:53.912 --> 00:12:55.120
PAWEL SIARKIEWICZ: Thank you.

00:12:55.120 --> 00:12:57.497
So just to finish
off this, if you

00:12:57.497 --> 00:12:59.080
would like to work
with Stadia, if you

00:12:59.080 --> 00:13:01.497
have a game idea that you'd
like to bring to our platform,

00:13:01.497 --> 00:13:03.160
then please head
over to stadia.dev,

00:13:03.160 --> 00:13:05.020
and we'd love to hear from you.

00:13:05.020 --> 00:13:07.160
And we're taking these
[? developer ?] applications

00:13:07.160 --> 00:13:07.660
now.

00:13:07.660 --> 00:13:10.210
And then, we'll be
getting back to you

00:13:10.210 --> 00:13:12.713
throughout the rest of the year.

00:13:12.713 --> 00:13:14.630
And also, come see us
at our booths and demos.

00:13:14.630 --> 00:13:15.920
If you actually
want to check out

00:13:15.920 --> 00:13:17.300
some of the tools,
how they work,

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:19.670
we're in the West Hall
for the developer section.

00:13:19.670 --> 00:13:22.105
And the South Hall has more
of the playability sections.

00:13:22.105 --> 00:13:23.480
And so you can
both experience it

00:13:23.480 --> 00:13:27.047
and see what it will look like
to actually build on Stadia.

00:13:27.047 --> 00:13:27.985
Thank you.

00:13:30.522 --> 00:13:31.480
MICAH BAKER: All right.

00:13:31.480 --> 00:13:34.450
So we're very excited to
continue this conversation

00:13:34.450 --> 00:13:37.460
offline if you have
any more questions.

00:13:37.460 --> 00:13:42.430
And next, I'd like to invite my
friend Paul up from Multiplay,

00:13:42.430 --> 00:13:44.840
where we're going to talk
a little bit about some

00:13:44.840 --> 00:13:46.460
of our successes
and working together

00:13:46.460 --> 00:13:49.372
on scaling their games.

00:13:49.372 --> 00:13:52.300
[APPLAUSE]

00:13:53.764 --> 00:13:55.810
PAUL MANUEL: Thank you.

00:13:55.810 --> 00:13:56.572
Thank you, Micah.

00:13:56.572 --> 00:13:57.530
Good morning, everyone.

00:13:57.530 --> 00:13:58.510
My name's Paul Manuel.

00:13:58.510 --> 00:14:01.600
I'm the managing
director of Multiplay.

00:14:01.600 --> 00:14:05.110
Thank you to Google for asking
us to come and talk today.

00:14:05.110 --> 00:14:08.230
What I'm going to be
touching on is, essentially,

00:14:08.230 --> 00:14:10.480
what Multiplay do to give
you a little bit of context,

00:14:10.480 --> 00:14:12.220
why we work so
closely with Google,

00:14:12.220 --> 00:14:15.230
and I'm going to talk about
quite an exciting case study,

00:14:15.230 --> 00:14:16.750
which we worked on this year.

00:14:19.714 --> 00:14:22.140
Click.

00:14:22.140 --> 00:14:22.640
OK.

00:14:22.640 --> 00:14:24.350
Just to frame it
up a little bit,

00:14:24.350 --> 00:14:26.767
I think it's worth explaining
a little bit about Multiplay

00:14:26.767 --> 00:14:28.320
and what we do.

00:14:28.320 --> 00:14:30.465
So we are game server
hosting specialists.

00:14:30.465 --> 00:14:32.090
And believe it or
not, we've been doing

00:14:32.090 --> 00:14:34.610
this for nearly 20 years now.

00:14:34.610 --> 00:14:36.880
So we have launched
a lot of games.

00:14:36.880 --> 00:14:40.050
And I wouldn't say we've seen
it all, but we've seen a lot.

00:14:40.050 --> 00:14:42.800
Now, we don't create games,
but we do power them.

00:14:42.800 --> 00:14:44.510
So effectively,
that is our calling.

00:14:44.510 --> 00:14:47.480
So a lot of you in the
audience create games.

00:14:47.480 --> 00:14:51.090
But our thing is we power
them through infrastructure.

00:14:51.090 --> 00:14:54.680
And it is our belief that
we have a responsibility

00:14:54.680 --> 00:14:57.620
to the game players of
the world when they invest

00:14:57.620 --> 00:15:01.680
either their money or their
valuable time to get on a game

00:15:01.680 --> 00:15:06.560
and be able to play it
quickly, fast, lag-free.

00:15:06.560 --> 00:15:09.260
And likewise, we also feel
like we have an obligation

00:15:09.260 --> 00:15:11.080
to the game developers.

00:15:11.080 --> 00:15:13.815
Whether you're a studio with
two people or 200 people,

00:15:13.815 --> 00:15:16.190
if you're going to invest two
or three years of your life

00:15:16.190 --> 00:15:19.190
into building the
most awesome game,

00:15:19.190 --> 00:15:21.040
then when it comes
to launch day,

00:15:21.040 --> 00:15:23.000
you've got to make
sure you get it right

00:15:23.000 --> 00:15:25.080
and all those customers
get to play your game.

00:15:25.080 --> 00:15:27.660
And that's our job.

00:15:27.660 --> 00:15:29.660
So we work hard on this.

00:15:29.660 --> 00:15:33.050
And we've been working with
Google Cloud for probably

00:15:33.050 --> 00:15:34.738
getting on near four years now.

00:15:34.738 --> 00:15:36.530
And I'll talk a little
bit more about that.

00:15:39.680 --> 00:15:44.550
Just to finish that story
and finish the framing,

00:15:44.550 --> 00:15:46.710
at the tail end
of 2017, Multiplay

00:15:46.710 --> 00:15:48.750
became part of the Unity family.

00:15:48.750 --> 00:15:50.940
So we're super
proud to join Unity.

00:15:50.940 --> 00:15:52.860
And if you know
anything about Unity,

00:15:52.860 --> 00:15:55.560
you know that they're the world
leading real-time creation

00:15:55.560 --> 00:15:56.370
platform.

00:15:56.370 --> 00:16:00.640
And they build awesome
tools for creating games.

00:16:00.640 --> 00:16:03.330
But what you may not know is
beyond the creation phase,

00:16:03.330 --> 00:16:06.180
as Unity, we now move into
the operational phase.

00:16:06.180 --> 00:16:08.100
And this is where
Multiplay sits.

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:11.280
So we provide the infrastructure
for large online multiplayer

00:16:11.280 --> 00:16:12.690
games.

00:16:12.690 --> 00:16:15.930
You'll hear soon more about
the Multiplay matchmaker.

00:16:15.930 --> 00:16:19.320
And we also have Vivox, who
are similar to Multiplay--

00:16:19.320 --> 00:16:21.840
they've joined the
Unity family recently--

00:16:21.840 --> 00:16:23.790
who power voice comms
for some of the largest

00:16:23.790 --> 00:16:25.950
games in the world, including
"Fortnite" and "PUBG"

00:16:25.950 --> 00:16:26.830
and so on.

00:16:26.830 --> 00:16:28.830
So the operational
phase is something

00:16:28.830 --> 00:16:30.510
that we do a great deal of.

00:16:30.510 --> 00:16:32.170
The important
message here, though,

00:16:32.170 --> 00:16:34.030
is we are engine agnostic.

00:16:34.030 --> 00:16:36.390
So at Multiplay,
we will host games

00:16:36.390 --> 00:16:39.240
regardless of what engine it
is, whether it's Unreal, Unity,

00:16:39.240 --> 00:16:43.250
or whatever, we work on those.

00:16:43.250 --> 00:16:44.078
So on to cloud.

00:16:44.078 --> 00:16:45.620
Let's talk about
cloud and the impact

00:16:45.620 --> 00:16:47.480
it has on the gaming industry.

00:16:47.480 --> 00:16:53.420
Well, it is true that cloud is
a game changer, that's for sure.

00:16:53.420 --> 00:16:55.130
You can now do
stuff that you never

00:16:55.130 --> 00:16:56.570
used to be able to do before.

00:16:56.570 --> 00:16:59.648
In the past, before
cloud existed,

00:16:59.648 --> 00:17:01.940
when you came to launch your
game, one of two scenarios

00:17:01.940 --> 00:17:03.260
would exist.

00:17:03.260 --> 00:17:06.980
You either had too much hardware
and only this many players

00:17:06.980 --> 00:17:10.369
turned up-- and that's an
expensive situation to be in--

00:17:10.369 --> 00:17:14.060
or, arguably even worse, you
have this much hardware--

00:17:14.060 --> 00:17:14.869
not enough--

00:17:14.869 --> 00:17:16.888
and this many players
turn up, in which case--

00:17:16.888 --> 00:17:17.930
and we've all been there.

00:17:17.930 --> 00:17:20.630
That's when you can't get into
the game, the most frustrating

00:17:20.630 --> 00:17:22.560
situation to be in.

00:17:22.560 --> 00:17:25.280
And it was a very, very
difficult problem to solve.

00:17:25.280 --> 00:17:26.780
And if you had that
second scenario,

00:17:26.780 --> 00:17:28.280
then you'd find
yourself scrambling,

00:17:28.280 --> 00:17:32.990
trying to get more servers
in, whereas cloud actually

00:17:32.990 --> 00:17:35.510
enables us to get rid of
that problem completely.

00:17:35.510 --> 00:17:37.610
I'm just going to talk
about three very typical--

00:17:37.610 --> 00:17:39.620
we've seen many, many
shapes of launches,

00:17:39.620 --> 00:17:42.150
but here's a few examples.

00:17:42.150 --> 00:17:45.710
This is the one we call the
highly anticipated launch, OK?

00:17:45.710 --> 00:17:48.260
So this is the game
that everybody knows.

00:17:48.260 --> 00:17:49.580
Everybody knows it's coming.

00:17:49.580 --> 00:17:52.550
And maybe it's a first-person
shooter that comes out just

00:17:52.550 --> 00:17:56.932
before Christmas every year,
or it's a seasonal sports title

00:17:56.932 --> 00:17:57.890
or something like that.

00:17:57.890 --> 00:17:58.890
So you know it's coming.

00:17:58.890 --> 00:18:01.790
Or maybe it's a massively
hyped new IP that everybody's

00:18:01.790 --> 00:18:04.160
super excited to hear about.

00:18:04.160 --> 00:18:05.788
And along comes
launch day, and that's

00:18:05.788 --> 00:18:06.830
what the peak looks like.

00:18:06.830 --> 00:18:08.747
And it looks like this
pretty much every time.

00:18:08.747 --> 00:18:10.980
And that peak is massive.

00:18:10.980 --> 00:18:15.060
And it lasts for a week or
two, or maybe a month or so,

00:18:15.060 --> 00:18:16.495
and then it dies down.

00:18:16.495 --> 00:18:17.870
And then, hopefully,
what happens

00:18:17.870 --> 00:18:19.953
is people stay engaged in
the game, the people who

00:18:19.953 --> 00:18:20.690
love the game.

00:18:20.690 --> 00:18:23.160
And then, you invest in it,
and it grows from there.

00:18:23.160 --> 00:18:25.130
So that's the
anticipated launch.

00:18:28.040 --> 00:18:30.137
Next slide.

00:18:30.137 --> 00:18:31.470
We also see quite a bit of this.

00:18:31.470 --> 00:18:34.900
And we have games that we host
at Multiplay exactly like this.

00:18:34.900 --> 00:18:37.830
So this is your games
as a service type model.

00:18:37.830 --> 00:18:39.780
They're seasonal.

00:18:39.780 --> 00:18:42.960
They will have large DLCs
or large events going on,

00:18:42.960 --> 00:18:45.030
large promotions,
large tournaments,

00:18:45.030 --> 00:18:47.760
or they have seasons, and
you'll see three or four spikes

00:18:47.760 --> 00:18:49.207
like this per year.

00:18:49.207 --> 00:18:51.540
The message to take away,
again, it's a different shape,

00:18:51.540 --> 00:18:55.060
but what's consistent is it's
very, very erratic and very

00:18:55.060 --> 00:18:55.560
spiky.

00:18:55.560 --> 00:18:57.310
And you have to have
enough infrastructure

00:18:57.310 --> 00:19:01.188
to cope with each one of those
spikes accurately every time.

00:19:01.188 --> 00:19:02.730
And the third example
I'll give you--

00:19:02.730 --> 00:19:05.990
and, possibly, for me, the
most exciting one of all--

00:19:05.990 --> 00:19:11.010
is the game that nobody knew
was going to come along,

00:19:11.010 --> 00:19:12.300
and it was a massive hit.

00:19:12.300 --> 00:19:15.540
So this is the unexpected hit.

00:19:15.540 --> 00:19:18.540
And we've seen this with "PUBG."

00:19:18.540 --> 00:19:22.050
We've seen this with "Fortnite,"
and we've seen it with

00:19:22.050 --> 00:19:26.190
the likes of "Pokémon GO" and,
more recently, "Apex Legends."

00:19:26.190 --> 00:19:29.040
And I'll talk a little bit
more about "Apex" in a minute.

00:19:29.040 --> 00:19:31.680
But what I can tell you
is an extreme version

00:19:31.680 --> 00:19:33.450
of that very graph
happened with "Apex

00:19:33.450 --> 00:19:35.900
Legends" in the first
week of its launch.

00:19:35.900 --> 00:19:37.050
And this is the tough one.

00:19:37.050 --> 00:19:38.800
Because this is the
one when you literally

00:19:38.800 --> 00:19:41.160
have no idea how many
players are going to turn up.

00:19:41.160 --> 00:19:43.830
But if a lot turn
up-- in the millions--

00:19:43.830 --> 00:19:46.320
then you need to be
absolutely sure you

00:19:46.320 --> 00:19:47.960
have the Cloud and
the infrastructure

00:19:47.960 --> 00:19:50.210
and the orchestration layer
ready to scale it to that.

00:19:53.450 --> 00:19:54.390
OK.

00:19:54.390 --> 00:19:56.700
So onto a little bit
of case study for you.

00:19:56.700 --> 00:19:58.950
As I said, we work with
many, many different games.

00:19:58.950 --> 00:20:00.540
We work with "Rocket League."

00:20:00.540 --> 00:20:02.332
We work with "PUBG."

00:20:02.332 --> 00:20:04.040
We work with Tripwire
on "Killing Floor."

00:20:04.040 --> 00:20:06.750
But we have a very special
relationship with our friends

00:20:06.750 --> 00:20:08.010
at Respawn.

00:20:08.010 --> 00:20:09.990
And going back a
couple of years,

00:20:09.990 --> 00:20:11.970
we did the launch
for "Titanfall 2."

00:20:11.970 --> 00:20:14.580
And collectively, this is where
we really honed our craft,

00:20:14.580 --> 00:20:18.090
I think, in terms of blending
multiple clouds and bare metal

00:20:18.090 --> 00:20:20.910
infrastructure into
one single fabric,

00:20:20.910 --> 00:20:24.630
enabling their matchmaker just
to talk to us via a single API.

00:20:24.630 --> 00:20:25.680
So they have a game.

00:20:25.680 --> 00:20:27.180
They say we have a
group of players.

00:20:27.180 --> 00:20:28.090
We're ready to go.

00:20:28.090 --> 00:20:29.760
We need a server on US West.

00:20:29.760 --> 00:20:30.830
Multiplay, can I have it?

00:20:30.830 --> 00:20:31.090
Boom.

00:20:31.090 --> 00:20:32.500
And we come back
with the server.

00:20:32.500 --> 00:20:35.250
What happens behind the scenes
they don't need to know about.

00:20:35.250 --> 00:20:36.450
It's magic to them.

00:20:36.450 --> 00:20:37.950
But as you can
imagine, it's a blend

00:20:37.950 --> 00:20:40.150
of multiple clouds
and bare metal.

00:20:40.150 --> 00:20:43.110
But there's a lot of
GCP in there, as well.

00:20:43.110 --> 00:20:45.360
So "Titanfall 2" was
a phenomenal launch.

00:20:45.360 --> 00:20:46.470
It was flawless.

00:20:46.470 --> 00:20:48.450
All the players got to play.

00:20:48.450 --> 00:20:53.040
The game had incredible reviews,
super, super high Metacritic

00:20:53.040 --> 00:20:54.060
rating.

00:20:54.060 --> 00:20:56.130
But the only shame, I
guess, in some respects,

00:20:56.130 --> 00:20:57.780
is not quite as many
players turned up

00:20:57.780 --> 00:21:00.510
as everybody had hoped for.

00:21:00.510 --> 00:21:04.210
However, fast forward
a couple years.

00:21:04.210 --> 00:21:08.780
And Respawn, once again,
in February of this year,

00:21:08.780 --> 00:21:10.180
we launched "Apex."

00:21:10.180 --> 00:21:12.160
The key difference
here, of course,

00:21:12.160 --> 00:21:15.550
is this game was free to play.

00:21:15.550 --> 00:21:18.700
And what actually happened
is in the first eight hours,

00:21:18.700 --> 00:21:20.940
we saw a million players.

00:21:20.940 --> 00:21:23.980
And then, in the first
72 hours, or three days,

00:21:23.980 --> 00:21:26.160
we saw 10 million
players that we served.

00:21:26.160 --> 00:21:27.860
And within the
first four weeks, we

00:21:27.860 --> 00:21:31.410
have saw 50 million players on
this game, each one of them,

00:21:31.410 --> 00:21:34.840
on the whole, always getting in
and having a great experience

00:21:34.840 --> 00:21:35.824
in "Apex Legends."

00:21:38.498 --> 00:21:40.290
And how do we prep for
something like that?

00:21:40.290 --> 00:21:42.260
Well, if you can see that
little video running,

00:21:42.260 --> 00:21:43.880
it's all about
preparation, actually.

00:21:43.880 --> 00:21:44.840
It's about testing.

00:21:44.840 --> 00:21:46.370
This stuff doesn't
happen by magic.

00:21:46.370 --> 00:21:50.420
This is us scaling up GCP
in advance, as a test,

00:21:50.420 --> 00:21:52.900
to an enormous number we
picked out of thin air--

00:21:52.900 --> 00:21:54.188
14 million players.

00:21:54.188 --> 00:21:55.980
And if you watch the
top right-hand corner,

00:21:55.980 --> 00:21:59.720
you will see that we spun up,
in a period of 90 minutes,

00:21:59.720 --> 00:22:03.410
enough capacity to serve 14
million concurrent players

00:22:03.410 --> 00:22:04.130
in a game.

00:22:04.130 --> 00:22:06.210
We weren't expecting
that amount of numbers,

00:22:06.210 --> 00:22:07.820
but we tested it
in advance, anyway.

00:22:07.820 --> 00:22:09.740
And what we were
testing here is not

00:22:09.740 --> 00:22:13.110
just GCP's ability to scale at
the pace that we needed it to.

00:22:13.110 --> 00:22:15.020
We were testing our
own core infrastructure

00:22:15.020 --> 00:22:18.470
to ensure that it could
take that volume of requests

00:22:18.470 --> 00:22:20.540
in that amount of
time, which it did.

00:22:20.540 --> 00:22:23.150
But the real key message
to take away from here

00:22:23.150 --> 00:22:25.490
is test in advance, OK?

00:22:25.490 --> 00:22:27.620
So as amazing as
Google Cloud is,

00:22:27.620 --> 00:22:30.170
or any cloud, and as amazing
as our own orchestration

00:22:30.170 --> 00:22:31.940
layer is that I
love to talk about,

00:22:31.940 --> 00:22:33.230
you still have a matchmaker.

00:22:33.230 --> 00:22:34.897
It still needs to
work in the right way.

00:22:34.897 --> 00:22:36.368
It needs to be stress tested.

00:22:36.368 --> 00:22:37.910
One of the great
things about working

00:22:37.910 --> 00:22:41.672
with a studio such as
Respawn, if you've ever

00:22:41.672 --> 00:22:43.130
played any of their
games, you will

00:22:43.130 --> 00:22:45.650
see that they are meticulous
in everything they do.

00:22:45.650 --> 00:22:47.750
And I can tell you from
firsthand experience,

00:22:47.750 --> 00:22:50.130
they are meticulous at
their testing, as well.

00:22:50.130 --> 00:22:51.052
So test in advance.

00:22:51.052 --> 00:22:52.010
Make sure you're ready.

00:22:52.010 --> 00:22:55.220
Work with a partner, so
when it comes to that day,

00:22:55.220 --> 00:22:57.950
having worked for all those
years of building your game,

00:22:57.950 --> 00:22:59.990
you are ready for
launch, and things work,

00:22:59.990 --> 00:23:01.190
and players get in.

00:23:01.190 --> 00:23:03.380
And that can make
or break a game.

00:23:03.380 --> 00:23:06.530
And I can tell you when it
goes wrong, Twitter lights up.

00:23:06.530 --> 00:23:09.022
Because I follow the
term server on Twitter.

00:23:09.022 --> 00:23:10.730
And I know what it
looks like when things

00:23:10.730 --> 00:23:13.000
go wrong and Twitter lights up.

00:23:13.000 --> 00:23:14.912
And it will be in the
thousands of people.

00:23:14.912 --> 00:23:17.120
Well, I can tell you in the
launch of "Apex Legends,"

00:23:17.120 --> 00:23:18.745
Twitter was very quiet.

00:23:18.745 --> 00:23:20.120
In fact, what was
wonderful about

00:23:20.120 --> 00:23:21.830
it is we saw a whole
bunch of tweets

00:23:21.830 --> 00:23:23.400
about people saying, wow.

00:23:23.400 --> 00:23:25.400
Has this game just launched
to this many players

00:23:25.400 --> 00:23:26.698
with no server problems?

00:23:26.698 --> 00:23:28.490
And that was a lovely,
lovely thing to see.

00:23:31.140 --> 00:23:35.610
So with "Apex Legends,"
we went from zero

00:23:35.610 --> 00:23:38.220
to hitting over two
million concurrent players

00:23:38.220 --> 00:23:39.960
in the first seven days.

00:23:39.960 --> 00:23:44.490
We spun up in GCP 6,500 VMs.

00:23:44.490 --> 00:23:46.500
And these are big
VMs, by the way.

00:23:46.500 --> 00:23:48.270
These are meaty machines.

00:23:48.270 --> 00:23:51.860
And that was across
53 locations globally.

00:23:51.860 --> 00:23:56.430
And the vast majority of the
game was in Google Cloud.

00:23:56.430 --> 00:24:00.120
So why do we work so
closely with Google?

00:24:00.120 --> 00:24:02.440
Well, actually, there's
a number of reasons.

00:24:02.440 --> 00:24:04.760
And I've called out four here.

00:24:04.760 --> 00:24:08.100
Winding back to when we
first looked at cloud,

00:24:08.100 --> 00:24:11.580
we benchmark, and we still, to
this day, use multiple clouds.

00:24:11.580 --> 00:24:13.238
But we benchmarked
Google, and one

00:24:13.238 --> 00:24:15.030
of the things that our
engineers loved was,

00:24:15.030 --> 00:24:17.740
actually, it was
very simple to use.

00:24:17.740 --> 00:24:20.010
In fact, in their
words, they found

00:24:20.010 --> 00:24:22.663
that they thought they had the
gold standard of documentation.

00:24:22.663 --> 00:24:25.080
And what it meant is we had
no experience in Google Cloud,

00:24:25.080 --> 00:24:26.663
and within a very
short space of time,

00:24:26.663 --> 00:24:29.640
we were up and running with
very little help from them

00:24:29.640 --> 00:24:32.980
in the sense that their
documentation was excellent.

00:24:32.980 --> 00:24:35.260
The other thing is
scale, of course.

00:24:35.260 --> 00:24:36.810
Google are continuing
to invest not

00:24:36.810 --> 00:24:39.445
only in their phenomenal
network but in terms

00:24:39.445 --> 00:24:41.820
of the amount of data centers
they have around the world.

00:24:41.820 --> 00:24:43.900
And they continue
that investment today.

00:24:43.900 --> 00:24:46.620
So we get global coverage.

00:24:46.620 --> 00:24:48.270
Performance was the
big one, though.

00:24:48.270 --> 00:24:50.040
What we really,
really cared about is

00:24:50.040 --> 00:24:51.810
from making a
request to a server,

00:24:51.810 --> 00:24:53.745
how quickly can we
get that server back?

00:24:53.745 --> 00:24:55.620
You can imagine when
you're scaling from zero

00:24:55.620 --> 00:24:57.180
to two million
concurrents, you need

00:24:57.180 --> 00:24:58.805
to be sure that you're
getting virtuals

00:24:58.805 --> 00:25:01.230
back very, very quickly.

00:25:01.230 --> 00:25:03.780
With "Apex Legends," we
will get a server back

00:25:03.780 --> 00:25:06.270
from a cold request
to up, run, and booted

00:25:06.270 --> 00:25:08.317
in less than two minutes.

00:25:08.317 --> 00:25:10.900
And then we do a little bit of
Multiplay magic on top of that,

00:25:10.900 --> 00:25:14.250
and then the server is in
the pool, ready to be used.

00:25:14.250 --> 00:25:17.200
To be super clear there, no
player waits for two minutes.

00:25:17.200 --> 00:25:19.440
So our algorithms at
Multiplay is constantly

00:25:19.440 --> 00:25:22.410
tracking the rate of change to
ensure that we have servers up

00:25:22.410 --> 00:25:24.720
and running and
warm, ready to go, as

00:25:24.720 --> 00:25:26.010
soon as the request comes in.

00:25:26.010 --> 00:25:28.020
So players were
in in an instant.

00:25:28.020 --> 00:25:29.833
But yeah, incredible
performance.

00:25:29.833 --> 00:25:31.500
And then, the last
one, which you rarely

00:25:31.500 --> 00:25:34.260
hear people talk about
when they talk about cloud

00:25:34.260 --> 00:25:36.150
is the people, actually.

00:25:36.150 --> 00:25:38.730
So we've had a phenomenal
partnership with Google

00:25:38.730 --> 00:25:39.482
from day one.

00:25:39.482 --> 00:25:41.190
And even when Multiplay
was a lot smaller

00:25:41.190 --> 00:25:44.160
than what it is
today, they really

00:25:44.160 --> 00:25:46.530
looked after us in terms
of testing stuff, doing

00:25:46.530 --> 00:25:48.745
unusual stuff, making
sure everything worked.

00:25:48.745 --> 00:25:50.370
And then, the launch
of "Apex Legends,"

00:25:50.370 --> 00:25:55.027
again, we were able to scale
to phenomenal amounts of VMs

00:25:55.027 --> 00:25:56.110
in a short amount of time.

00:25:56.110 --> 00:25:58.230
And that's because we
had Google Cloud working

00:25:58.230 --> 00:26:01.110
very, very closely with us to
make sure we were well-prepared

00:26:01.110 --> 00:26:01.900
for this project.

00:26:01.900 --> 00:26:04.830
So the partnership, in terms of
the people, is absolutely key.

00:26:07.600 --> 00:26:14.400
So final wrap-up for me, really,
is, I guess, for you guys--

00:26:14.400 --> 00:26:17.160
you know this-- making games
is a very, very difficult thing

00:26:17.160 --> 00:26:18.370
to do.

00:26:18.370 --> 00:26:21.030
So it would be great if
you just had one less

00:26:21.030 --> 00:26:22.040
thing to worry about.

00:26:22.040 --> 00:26:24.280
And for me, I think
it's the infrastructure.

00:26:24.280 --> 00:26:26.940
So if there's one thing you
take away from this talk today,

00:26:26.940 --> 00:26:29.580
is that we know that
creativity is the beating

00:26:29.580 --> 00:26:32.620
heart of any game studio.

00:26:32.620 --> 00:26:34.840
But you don't need to worry
about the infrastructure

00:26:34.840 --> 00:26:35.590
logistics.

00:26:35.590 --> 00:26:37.780
Those problems have
been solved by companies

00:26:37.780 --> 00:26:41.110
like us many, many, many,
many times over, OK?

00:26:41.110 --> 00:26:45.780
And here's a quote from
John Shiring from Respawn.

00:26:45.780 --> 00:26:48.460
He says, "For the launch of
'Apex Legends,' it was critical

00:26:48.460 --> 00:26:53.320
both that we could scale our
servers up to handle whatever

00:26:53.320 --> 00:26:56.928
size audience showed up and that
those servers were reliable.

00:26:56.928 --> 00:26:58.720
We continued our
partnership with Multiplay

00:26:58.720 --> 00:27:01.090
to leverage their
multi-cloud auto-scaler

00:27:01.090 --> 00:27:04.690
so we can focus our developer
time on the game itself

00:27:04.690 --> 00:27:07.120
and not on keeping the
game servers running."

00:27:07.120 --> 00:27:09.220
That's absolutely key.

00:27:09.220 --> 00:27:11.230
John Shiring, not
just from Respawn

00:27:11.230 --> 00:27:14.040
but from "Call of
Duty," "Infinity Ward

00:27:14.040 --> 00:27:16.540
Call of Duty," "Modern
Warfare," and all those games.

00:27:16.540 --> 00:27:18.533
These are very
experienced people,

00:27:18.533 --> 00:27:20.200
and they've made the
decision, actually,

00:27:20.200 --> 00:27:22.480
let's let someone else look
after the infrastructure.

00:27:22.480 --> 00:27:25.372
We'll concentrate on the game.

00:27:25.372 --> 00:27:26.080
That's it for me.

00:27:26.080 --> 00:27:26.913
Thank you very much.

00:27:26.913 --> 00:27:28.367
I'm going to hand back to Micah.

00:27:28.367 --> 00:27:31.169
[APPLAUSE]

00:27:34.450 --> 00:27:35.740
MICAH BAKER: Thank you, Paul.

00:27:35.740 --> 00:27:38.980
One of my favorite things
about Multiplay is the people.

00:27:38.980 --> 00:27:41.110
Technology is great,
but people are what

00:27:41.110 --> 00:27:42.580
make the relationships work.

00:27:42.580 --> 00:27:45.000
And Multiplay excels
at relationships,

00:27:45.000 --> 00:27:47.330
so we're really pleased
to work with them.

00:27:47.330 --> 00:27:49.150
I also wanted to
mention that one

00:27:49.150 --> 00:27:53.200
of the benefits of using, for
example, Multiplay or hosting

00:27:53.200 --> 00:27:55.900
games on GCP, in general,
along with Stadia,

00:27:55.900 --> 00:27:58.920
is that you're in a perfect
position to be cross-platform.

00:27:58.920 --> 00:28:01.690
So you'll be able to host
your game servers in one place

00:28:01.690 --> 00:28:04.540
and service all the
different screens that you

00:28:04.540 --> 00:28:06.430
want to get your game to.

00:28:06.430 --> 00:28:08.740
So it's my pleasure to
invite our next speaker up,

00:28:08.740 --> 00:28:13.430
Rami Ismail, who will be talking
to us about our developers

00:28:13.430 --> 00:28:14.938
and global diversity.

00:28:20.914 --> 00:28:23.902
[APPLAUSE]

00:28:25.143 --> 00:28:25.810
RAMI ISMAIL: Hi.

00:28:28.430 --> 00:28:30.890
As a game developer,
you quickly come

00:28:30.890 --> 00:28:34.010
to realize that play
is a global language.

00:28:34.010 --> 00:28:36.080
It is something that
people understand,

00:28:36.080 --> 00:28:39.350
regardless of their
culture, their language,

00:28:39.350 --> 00:28:40.940
or their heritage.

00:28:40.940 --> 00:28:43.780
If you put a ball on the ground,
no matter where in the world

00:28:43.780 --> 00:28:45.920
you are and you kick
it towards somebody,

00:28:45.920 --> 00:28:48.290
you'll find that
they kick it back.

00:28:48.290 --> 00:28:51.375
Over the past decade or so,
I've traveled around the world

00:28:51.375 --> 00:28:53.000
to meet and assist
game developers that

00:28:53.000 --> 00:28:56.000
work in the widest possible
variety of circumstances,

00:28:56.000 --> 00:28:59.810
from developers that work
in the ice-cold north

00:28:59.810 --> 00:29:01.460
and a month-long
night to developers

00:29:01.460 --> 00:29:04.130
that are making games
on a cliff overlooking

00:29:04.130 --> 00:29:07.088
the plains of Africa.

00:29:07.088 --> 00:29:08.630
And during those
travels, I've always

00:29:08.630 --> 00:29:10.547
aimed to assist developers
with the challenges

00:29:10.547 --> 00:29:13.670
unique to their country, access
to knowledge, to experience,

00:29:13.670 --> 00:29:16.360
to press, funding,
and to technology.

00:29:16.360 --> 00:29:19.280
And it has been my career
goal to democratize access

00:29:19.280 --> 00:29:22.020
to our technology, to our
libraries of knowledge,

00:29:22.020 --> 00:29:23.726
and to our industry.

00:29:23.726 --> 00:29:27.270
To further that goal, a group of
talented games industry members

00:29:27.270 --> 00:29:32.020
and myself have
founded gamedev.world,

00:29:32.020 --> 00:29:34.950
which is an online,
livestreamed, multilingual

00:29:34.950 --> 00:29:36.025
games conference.

00:29:36.025 --> 00:29:37.650
And as we gather here
in San Francisco,

00:29:37.650 --> 00:29:40.732
it is worth considering the
privilege and opportunity

00:29:40.732 --> 00:29:42.690
that events like the Game
Developers Conference

00:29:42.690 --> 00:29:46.920
offer creators that are Western
or that speak English or that

00:29:46.920 --> 00:29:49.342
have economical access
to these events.

00:29:49.342 --> 00:29:53.570
So from June 21 until June 23,
gamedev.world will livestream

00:29:53.570 --> 00:29:57.020
for free game developer talks
in eight major languages

00:29:57.020 --> 00:29:57.740
of the world--

00:29:57.740 --> 00:30:01.370
English, French, Spanish,
Brazilian Portuguese, Japanese,

00:30:01.370 --> 00:30:04.670
simplified Chinese,
Russian, and Arabic.

00:30:04.670 --> 00:30:06.410
Speakers from all
over the world,

00:30:06.410 --> 00:30:08.140
speaking in their
native tongues,

00:30:08.140 --> 00:30:10.850
will share their knowledge,
perspectives, and stories.

00:30:10.850 --> 00:30:13.880
And for the first time in the
history of the games industry,

00:30:13.880 --> 00:30:16.460
we'll be able to share that
knowledge across the language

00:30:16.460 --> 00:30:19.920
barrier through live subtitling.

00:30:19.920 --> 00:30:22.960
The internet was touted as a
globally connective technology,

00:30:22.960 --> 00:30:25.650
a technology that would allow
us to reach beyond our cities,

00:30:25.650 --> 00:30:29.830
to speak across our borders, and
to create for global audiences.

00:30:29.830 --> 00:30:31.590
It has achieved
many of those goals,

00:30:31.590 --> 00:30:35.180
but many challenges and
opportunities remain.

00:30:35.180 --> 00:30:37.820
So as we advance
through 2019, it

00:30:37.820 --> 00:30:40.610
is critical to acknowledge
that major technologies don't

00:30:40.610 --> 00:30:44.400
support non-Latin languages
in their software or websites,

00:30:44.400 --> 00:30:48.000
that global often means
everywhere but Africa,

00:30:48.000 --> 00:30:51.170
and that inclusivity is often
a shortcut for inclusivity

00:30:51.170 --> 00:30:53.210
for those who speak English.

00:30:53.210 --> 00:30:56.690
It is important to realize that
through technology, diversity,

00:30:56.690 --> 00:30:59.870
and proactive efforts, we
can challenge and defeat

00:30:59.870 --> 00:31:02.330
many of those notions
if only we consider

00:31:02.330 --> 00:31:05.660
that the internet is larger
than the Latin alphabet,

00:31:05.660 --> 00:31:07.310
that access to
high-speed internet

00:31:07.310 --> 00:31:10.220
is still not ubiquitous, and
that language and access are

00:31:10.220 --> 00:31:13.370
some of the largest invisible
privileges all of us

00:31:13.370 --> 00:31:16.075
in this room share.

00:31:16.075 --> 00:31:17.700
We've seen the internet
connect players

00:31:17.700 --> 00:31:19.830
all over the world to
play games together,

00:31:19.830 --> 00:31:22.470
despite not sharing a language.

00:31:22.470 --> 00:31:25.110
We've seen how global
communities solve problems

00:31:25.110 --> 00:31:28.980
together, create beautiful
content in games together,

00:31:28.980 --> 00:31:32.430
and play together,
despite never meeting.

00:31:32.430 --> 00:31:34.280
The games industry
has pioneered ways

00:31:34.280 --> 00:31:37.410
of play, ways of
connectivity, that

00:31:37.410 --> 00:31:41.050
are unrestrained by language
and universal in their reach.

00:31:41.050 --> 00:31:43.740
So as I travel the world to meet
the game development community

00:31:43.740 --> 00:31:46.170
in emerging territories--
sometimes half a dozen

00:31:46.170 --> 00:31:48.870
creators, sometimes
hundreds of them--

00:31:48.870 --> 00:31:51.630
I see how open-source
technology and free tool sets

00:31:51.630 --> 00:31:53.910
have democratized our industry.

00:31:53.910 --> 00:31:57.360
I see how knowledge sharing
takes a little bit of time

00:31:57.360 --> 00:32:01.090
but affects thousands of careers
and lives across the world.

00:32:01.090 --> 00:32:02.670
I see how community
translation makes

00:32:02.670 --> 00:32:05.960
information available in one
language available in another.

00:32:05.960 --> 00:32:09.030
And I've seen how making
issues of access and issues

00:32:09.030 --> 00:32:11.610
of opportunity more visible
through the internet

00:32:11.610 --> 00:32:13.620
has enabled our
industry to respond

00:32:13.620 --> 00:32:18.000
to challenges we would otherwise
be entirely unaware of.

00:32:18.000 --> 00:32:21.100
At gamedev.world, we
think about those truths

00:32:21.100 --> 00:32:22.822
as we prepare for the event.

00:32:22.822 --> 00:32:24.280
We're optimists
about the potential

00:32:24.280 --> 00:32:25.750
of technology in the world.

00:32:25.750 --> 00:32:28.300
We are optimists about
the role of the internet

00:32:28.300 --> 00:32:30.590
as connective tissue
between humans.

00:32:30.590 --> 00:32:32.740
We are optimists
about equalizing

00:32:32.740 --> 00:32:35.290
opportunity and access
through the networks that

00:32:35.290 --> 00:32:37.110
already exist.

00:32:37.110 --> 00:32:41.060
And we believe that progress
is both to dream about what

00:32:41.060 --> 00:32:43.580
isn't possible yet
and to critically

00:32:43.580 --> 00:32:47.160
examine what is available
to only a few of us.

00:32:47.160 --> 00:32:50.050
We have found the same values
and that same enthusiasm

00:32:50.050 --> 00:32:52.340
and optimism in the
Google Cloud team.

00:32:52.340 --> 00:32:54.820
We are extremely grateful
for their support

00:32:54.820 --> 00:32:57.500
as a diamond sponsor
for gamedev.world.

00:32:57.500 --> 00:33:01.450
Our goal is to, even
if just for one moment,

00:33:01.450 --> 00:33:03.580
shatter the language
barrier and show

00:33:03.580 --> 00:33:05.860
the medium and industry
of games as it truly

00:33:05.860 --> 00:33:10.150
is-- a global force, a universal
language not constrained

00:33:10.150 --> 00:33:13.180
by the language we speak,
the place we are born,

00:33:13.180 --> 00:33:15.010
or the access we have.

00:33:15.010 --> 00:33:18.640
As the gamedev.world team
works to present the first ever

00:33:18.640 --> 00:33:20.620
international games
conference, we

00:33:20.620 --> 00:33:23.420
hope that you will sign up for
the conference at gamedev.world

00:33:23.420 --> 00:33:28.000
and that you will join us live
on the internet on June 21.

00:33:28.000 --> 00:33:29.850
Thanks so much.

